<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stono Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stono Rebellion was a revolt of enslaved Africans that began on 9 September 1739 near the Stono River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. Led by a man named Jemmy, many of the rebels came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>; they killed about 20 colonists and marched towards Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, which offered freedom, before militia defeated them. It was the largest slave revolt in the British mainland colonies, and the Negro Act of 1740 imposed harsher controls.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since 1708 enslaved Africans had made up the majority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>'s population, as planters imported more captives for the expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> crops; the historian Ira Berlin called the colony a slave society rather than a society with slaves. Many of the enslaved came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, whose rulers had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1491, and some had first been sold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> in particular. Civil wars in Kongo in the early 18th century had sent trained soldiers into the trade, and the historian John K. Thornton holds that Jemmy and his first followers were probably such men.</p><p>Successive governors of Spanish Florida promised freedom to fugitives who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and served in the militia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a> was set up near St. Augustine to house them. A malaria epidemic had recently killed many whites in Charleston. The Security Act of 1739, which required white men to carry arms even to church, had passed in August but could only be enforced after 29 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On Wednesday, 9 September 1739, Jemmy gathered about twenty men near the Stono River, 20 miles (30 km) south-west of Charleston. Marching under a banner reading Liberty and chanting the word, they attacked Hutchinson's store at the Stono River Bridge, killed two storekeepers and took weapons and ammunition. The historian Mark M. Smith links the timing, the day after the Feast of the Nativity of Mary, to their Catholic past.</p><p>Heading south, they gathered recruits, some unwilling, to a total of 81, and burned six plantations. Lieutenant Governor William Bull and five companions came upon them on horseback and rode off to raise the alarm. A mounted militia of planters caught 76 of the rebels at the Edisto River, where 47 of them were killed. Severed heads were set on stakes along the main roads, and Chickasaw and Catawba trackers were hired to hunt the survivors, some of whom fought a last battle about 30 miles (50 km) away a week later. Most captives were executed; the rest were sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Blaming newly imported Africans, planters imposed a ten-year halt on importing captives through Charleston, and afterwards bought from regions other than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. The Negro Act of 1740 required one white to every ten blacks on a plantation and forbade the enslaved to grow their own food, assemble, earn money or learn to read. Manumission now needed the legislature's consent in each case, a rule kept until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> ended after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. The Hutchinson's warehouse site was declared a National Historic Landmark in 1974.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stono Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Stono Rebellion</b> of 1739 led to harsh new slave laws.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-south-carolina">Province of South Carolina</a></li></ul></section>
